1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The seafarers saw — a map
without a compass.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
From the Pacific to the Indian Ocean,
sailors steered by the stars.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Oldest surviving written record: the
Odyssey, 2,700 years ago. A goddess
says: keep the Great Bear on your left.

4
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The first Chinese record of a compass at
sea, 900 years ago: used only on cloudy
days.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
1976: Micronesian navigator Mau Piailug
took the canoe Hōkūleʻa to Tahiti, no
instruments.

6
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Hawaiians later split the horizon into
32 houses. Arcturus straight overhead
means you’ve reached Hawaiʻi, 19° N.

7
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
15th century: navigator Ibn Mājid
already had a magnetic needle on board.

8
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
But the 32 compass points still bore
star names: mostly where bright stars
rise or set.

9
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
All night, the Pole Star sits about as
high as your latitude.

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Arabic lunar stations: where the Moon
stays each night.

11
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

